<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Government of National Salvation</h1><p class="st-meta">1941 CE – 1944 CE · capital Belgrade</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Collaborationist Serbian government of General Milan Nedić, which administered German-occupied Serbia during the Second World War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> conquest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia</a> in April 1941, a reduced <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> was placed under a German military commander. A commissioner government led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-acimovic">Milan Aćimović</a> proved unable to cope with the uprising of summer 1941, and on 29 August 1941 the Germans installed the former war minister General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-nedic">Milan Nedić</a> at the head of a Government of National Salvation.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The government ran the civil administration, police and a Serbian State Guard, but the German occupiers held real power and carried out mass reprisals, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kragujevac-massacre">Kragujevac massacre</a> of October 1941. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> were murdered in 1941 and 1942, with the help of local police. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-nedic">Nedić</a> promoted a conservative peasant ideology and sheltered refugees from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Partisans</a> and the Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> advanced, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-nedic">Nedić</a> fled in October 1944 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> was liberated. He died in Yugoslav custody in 1946. The regime's record of collaboration remains debated in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side">